Nintendo's always giving people choices  when it comes to handhelds so it comes  as no surprise to see new 3ds models on  the horizon yet the introduction of  Nintendo's new 3ds feels different it's  not just about size or value they're  practically the same size is the  original 30s in 3dsxl but they're  technically superior in every way in a  market where that's the case maybe the  better question is where do the new 3ds  systems leave the old models the answer  in their dust  let's start from the top for the first  time since I started using a 3ds four  years ago I feel not just comfortable  but excited when using the system's 3d  feature by installing a second camera  above the new 3ds XL top screen Nintendo  made it possible for the system to  detect your relative position and adjust  the 3d effect accordingly it's such a  drastic improvement over previous models  that once you see in action it's  impossible to go back to the 3d of old  there are two new buttons ZL and Zr  stuffed between the standard l and r  shoulder buttons on the new 3ds XL and  antennas also crammed a small stiff  analog nub between the face buttons and  the bottom screen though it looks like  it might be uncomfortable and less  flexible than the standard 3d SC stick  the new analog input is surprisingly  responsive this addition will open the  door for developers to create better  camera controls you can still get these  features on an older 3ds or 3ds XL but  you need to purchase and attach the  bulky Circle Pad Pro add-on to do so yes  there will be games that are exclusive  to new 3d s in the future and it's  because of one new feature the faster  CPU with a slight uptick in performance  it should allow developers to push the  graphics hardware a little further  without fear of bottlenecking  performance having played Xenoblade  Chronicles on new 3ds it's not  surprising that the game needs the extra  oomph to run as well as it does however  you're more likely going to appreciate  the effect the faster CPU has on the  system as a whole the OS is snappier  than ever in games download and load  faster the 3ds OS no longer feels  sluggish are too demanding for the  hardware now it's just right intend has  made some great additions for new 3ds  but it's also changed a few things that  aren't necessarily for the better  up until now 3ds store data on SD cards  but new 3ds systems require microSD  cards instead which are much smaller by  comparison as a result existing 3d s  owners need to purchase a new card or  settle for the included four gigabyte  microSD card which is enough to get you  started but it's probably too small for  long-term storage needs in the past the  SD slot was readily available but now  you need to unscrew and dismantle the  underside of new 3dsxl is to access the  microSD slot granted this isn't  something you need to do maybe more than  once or twice during the lifespan your  system but when you do you'll encounter  stubborn screws and a panel that  practically refuses to detach it's  inconvenient to say the least but this  setup does allow for a great new feature  the ability to replace the system's  internal battery thankfully new 3d S  touts slightly improved  which will result in less wear and tear  over time as you deplete and recharge  the battery you can expect about 30  minutes of extra battery life compared  to older 3dsxl models intentos also  remix the layout of activity lights  ports and switches on the new 3ds start  and selector now discrete circular  buttons that live beneath the ABXY  buttons underneath the bottom screen the  home button sets a look the front of new  3ds XL is where you'll find the game  card slot a headphone port stylus and  the power button which have all been  repositioned in some way but something's  missing  the wireless radio switch it's a minor  inconvenience but you now need to press  the home button and access the quick  settings menu to turn the wireless  communications on or off of course  there's a lot of buzz in the nintendo  world about amiibo at the moment  Nintendo's NFC enabled figures are not  just cute but also functional allowing  you to carry your personal game data  from one device to another unlike past  3ds models new 3d s's have a built-in  NFC radio amiibo support for 3ds games  is basically non-existent at launch but  expect that to change quickly  Nintendo's promised an external amiibo  reading add-on for existing 3ds hardware  but new 3ds owners will be ready for  amiibo right out of the box there's much  to love about the new 3ds but Nintendo's  made one decision that will prove to be  an inconvenience for first-time 3ds  owners it's decided not to include a  charger in the box if you like me then  planning 3ds for years the overall  difference when you pick up a new 3ds XL  is immediately apparent after week with  a new system I just can't imagine going  back the 3d is too good and it's great  to see the OS get a speed boost anyone  who's picking up a 3ds for the first  time should skip the other options and  go right for the new 3ds XL because  picking up any other version is  essentially setting yourself up for  disappointment down the road once  exclusive games start to roll out the  existing 3ds owners should also consider  an upgrade if not now perhaps when  they're motivated by a certain game that  said if you spend a lot of time with  your 3ds and want a better experience  you won't be disappointed if you rush  out on a February 13th to pick up the  best handheld Nintendo's ever made
 

